1 BACKUP
    The BACKUP command works with VMS BACKUP save sets, with VMS's syntax:
    it lists a save set, restores files from one, compares one with the
    files it was made from, and saves files into a new one.  A save set
    may be a file on a mounted volume, as VMS keeps one, or a host file
    holding the same bytes (a binary FTP of a save set, or COPY/BINARY of
    one).

    It follows VMS V7.1's BACKUP V6.2: the save sets it writes are laid
    out as that VMS lays them out, and VMS reads them.  Image, physical,
    and incremental operations, tapes, and copies of files to files are
    not done.

    Syntax:

        BACKUP/LIST[=file] save-set/SAVE_SET
        BACKUP save-set/SAVE_SET output-spec
        BACKUP/COMPARE save-set/SAVE_SET file-spec
        BACKUP file-spec[,...] save-set/SAVE_SET

    A save set on a disk must have /SAVE_SET.  Which specifier is the save
    set says what BACKUP does: as input, a restore (or with /COMPARE, a
    compare); as output, a save.  /LIST alone lists the input save set.

    Where a qualifier stands says what it is about, as in VMS: after BACKUP
    it is a command qualifier; after the input specifier, an input
    save-set or input file-selection qualifier; after the output
    specifier, an output save-set or output file qualifier.

    Command qualifiers:

        /LIST[=file]    List the save set, on SYS$OUTPUT or into the file
                        (its type LIS unless it gives one).  As in VMS
                        V7.1, a listing shows every file: /SELECT and
                        /EXCLUDE don't change it.
        /BRIEF, /FULL   The listing's layout: each file's name, used
                        blocks, and creation date (the default), or a
                        block of each file's dates, owner, file ID,
                        protection, and record attributes.
        /LOG            Show each file as it is saved, restored, or
                        compared.
        /COMPARE        Compare the save set's files with the files named.
        /IGNORE=NOBACKUP
                        Save the data of files marked NOBACKUP too.
                        Without it such a file's header alone is saved.
        /IGNORE=INTERLOCK
                        Save a file another process has open for writing,
                        as it stands.  Without it such a file isn't saved.

    Input save-set qualifiers:

        /SAVE_SET       The input is a save set.
        /CRC            Check each block's CRC, recovering a damaged block
                        from its redundancy group.  As in VMS V7.1, the
                        default is /NOCRC; a block whose header is damaged
                        is recovered either way.
        /SELECT=(file-spec[,...])
                        Only the save set's files these names match.
        /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])
                        Not the files these names match.
        /HOST           The save set is a host file, whatever its name.

    Input file-selection qualifiers (a save):

        /EXCLUDE=(file-spec[,...])
                        Not the files these names match (a directory file
                        is never excluded).
        /BY_OWNER[=uic] Only files with this owner (yours, with no UIC).
        /SINCE=time, /BEFORE=time
                        Only files dated since or before the time (TODAY,
                        YESTERDAY, TOMORROW, or a VMS time), by the date
                        one of /MODIFIED (the default), /CREATED,
                        /EXPIRED, or /BACKUP names.

    Output save-set qualifiers (a save):

        /SAVE_SET       The output is a save set.
        /BLOCK_SIZE=n   The save set's block size in bytes, 2048 to 65535,
                        rounded up to a multiple of 512 (32256).
        /GROUP_SIZE=n   Blocks in each redundancy group (10; 0 for none).
        /CRC            Write each block's CRC (the default is /NOCRC).
        /COMMENT=string Put the text in the save set's summary.
        /BY_OWNER=uic   The save set file's owner.
        /HOST           Write the save set as a host file.

    Output file qualifiers (a restore):

        /NEW_VERSION    Restore a file that exists as a new version.
        /OVERLAY        Write over a file that exists, in place.
        /REPLACE        Delete a file that exists, and restore it again.
                        With none of the three, a file that exists isn't
                        restored.
        /BY_OWNER[=option]
                        The restored files' owner: DEFAULT (yours, the
                        default without /BY_OWNER), ORIGINAL (the saved
                        owner, the default with /BY_OWNER alone), PARENT
                        (the directory's), or a UIC.

    The output specifier of a restore names the directory the files go
    to: [*...] restores each file to the directory it was saved from,
    [dir...] to a tree under [dir], and [dir] to that one directory (no
    directory, or [], is the default directory).  Under [dir...] a file
    keeps its whole directory ([B69.SUB1]A.TXT to [dir.B69.SUB1]), or
    with /SELECT the part the selection's "..." matched:
    /SELECT=[B69...] restores [B69.SUB1]A.TXT to [dir.SUB1].  A name or
    type in the output specifier renames the files; each keeps its saved
    version.  Directories that don't exist are made.  A host directory
    may be the output too ("out", or "out/..." for a tree); its files are
    written as COPY writes them.

    A save goes through each directory in order, every version of each
    file unless the specifier names one, and with [dir...] into each
    subdirectory, which is saved too, where its directory file is.  Each
    element of an input list starts in a new save set block, and takes
    the device and directory of the element before it when it names
    none.

    A restored file keeps its saved attributes: record format, dates,
    revision count, protection, characteristics, version limit,
    allocation, and access control list.  A file the save set has only
    part of is restored with zeros for the blocks missing (VBNMISSING).

    Examples:

        BACKUP/LIST DUA1:[SETS]B69.BCK/SAVE_SET
        BACKUP/LOG DUA1:[SETS]B69.BCK/SAVE_SET/SELECT=[B69]*.TXT [R1...]
        BACKUP/LIST "./pi.bck"/SAVE_SET
        BACKUP [WORK...]*.* DUA1:[SETS]WORK.BCK/SAVE_SET
